\section{Entities and Attributes}
The database design for Katikafwe Private School encompasses a comprehensive set of entities that collectively model the institution's academic and administrative ecosystem. Centered around the core actor, the student, the design ensures that every entity either directly interacts or influences the student’s academic journey ntities with no direct or functional relevance to the student were excluded to maintain schema efficiency and data integrity. The final set of entities includes Grade, Class, Teacher, Student, Subject, Guardian, Test\_Result, Term, Student\_Guardian, Class\_Subject, Fee\_Structure, and Fee\_Transaction, each fulfilling a distinct operational role within the educational management framework.

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Student Entity}

The Student entity serves as the central component of the schema, capturing all relevant information about learners. Attributes include student\_id, first\_name, last\_name, national\_id, date\_of\_birth, gender, status, enrollment\_date, enrollment\_time, and address. Although \textit{first\_name} and \textit{last\_name} could conceptually be multi-valued, as individuals may possess multiple given names or double-barreled surnames, they are implemented as single-valued attributes to simplify queries and data indexing while maintaining readability during retrieval operations. Since each student belongs to a class, therefore class\_id serves as a foreign key that points to the Class entity. The derived attribute, age, is calculated as the difference between the current date and the date\_of\_birth so avoid data redundancy. Address was initially a complex attribute. However, it was simplified into a single-valued field to enhance query performance and normalization consistency between related entities. Constraints for student are that student belongs to exactly one class and has at least one guardian.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Student} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{student\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{class\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{national\_id} & VARCHAR(20) \\
\texttt{first\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{last\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{date\_of\_birth} & DATE \\
\texttt{gender} & ENUM('Male','Female') \\
\texttt{status} & ENUM('Active','Transferred','Graduated') \\
\texttt{enrollment\_date} & DATE \\
\texttt{enrollment\_time} & TIME \\
\texttt{age} & INT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:student-entity}
\end{table}


\subsection{Guardian Entity}
 The guardian entity is a weak entity, since its existence is dependent on the student entity. It includes attributes such as guardian\_id, first\_name, last\_name, relationship\_to\_student, and contact. Contact is a complex attribute that branches into address, phone\_number and email. Address is a composite attribute that further branches into state, city and postal Code. Phone\_number is a multi-valued attribute that recognizes the possession of multiple contact numbers from guardian a parent.Constraint is each student must have at least one guardian but one guardian can be linked to more than one student.
\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Guardian} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{guardian\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{student\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{first\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{last\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{relationship\_to\_student} & VARCHAR(30) \\
\texttt{phone\_number} & VARCHAR(20) \\
\texttt{email} & VARCHAR(100) \\
\texttt{address} & VARCHAR(255) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:guardian-entity}
\end{table}


 %% A subsection
\subsection{Grade Entity}
The Grade entity represents the academic levels within the school. Its attributes being the grade\_id (primary key), grade\_level (ranging from 1 to 12) and teacher\_id (foreign key referencing the Teacher entity).Validation constraints will ensure that the grade\_level remains within the school’s academic range. It will also ensure that each grade is assigned a teacher supervisor. Each grade also has exactly two classes. In addition, students automatically progress to the next grade if they are in a grade less than 12. Grade 12 students become graduates. Assumption for the system is that each grade pays the same amount of fees.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Grade} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{grade\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{grade\_level} & INT \\
\texttt{teacher\_id} & INT \\
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:grade-entity}
\end{table}
 %% A subsection
\subsection{Class Entity}
The Class entity represents individual classroom units within each grade. Attributes include the class\_id (primary key), class\_teacher\_id (foreign key from Teacher),class\_name, room\_number, and capacity. Capacity is a composite attribute that is subdivided into max\_capacity and current-capacity. This is to ensure that the facilitation administration monitors the enrollment limits.A unique constraint on room\_number prevents duplication and aids in spatial management. The constraint is that each grade must have two classes. Each class has defined mandatory subjects and selective subjects have enrollment limits.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Class} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{class\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{grade\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{class\_teacher\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{class\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{room\_number} & VARCHAR(10) \\
\texttt{capacity} & INT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:class-entity}
\end{table}

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Teacher Entity}
·       The Teacher entity stores information about school faculty members responsible for teaching and class administration. Attributes include teacher\_id, first\_name, last\_name, gender, date\_of\_birth, contact, role, employment\_date, employment\_time and age. The contact attribute mirrors that of the Guardian entity. Both employment\_time and age are derived attributes calculated at runtime to avoid redundancy. Employment\_time mainly capturing the number of years a teacher has been employed in the school. The attribute of the role captures the administrative or instructional designation of the teacher, for example, principal, senior teacher, or head of department. The constraint is that a teacher can serve multiple roles. Teachers can only be grade or class teacher once per year.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Teacher} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{teacher\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{first\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{last\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{gender} & ENUM('Male','Female') \\
\texttt{date\_of\_birth} & DATE \\
\texttt{contact} & VARCHAR(255) \\
\texttt{role} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{employment\_date} & DATE \\
\texttt{employment\_time} & INT \\
\texttt{age} & INT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\label{tab:teacher-entity}
\end{table}


 %% A subsection
\subsection{Term Entity}
The Term entity represents academic periods. Its attributes include term\_id, academic\_year, term\_number (values 1–3), term\_name, start\_date, and end\_date. Validation ensures chronological consistency and prevents overlapping term durations. This entity provides a temporal link across academic records such as grades, subjects, and financial transactions. The constraint used is that each academic year has a fixed number of terms, three.
\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Term} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{term\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{academic\_year} & YEAR \\
\texttt{term\_number} & ENUM('1','2','3') \\
\texttt{term\_name} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{start\_date} & DATE \\
\texttt{end\_date} & DATE \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:term-entity}
\end{table}

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Fee\_Structure Entity}
The Fee\_Structure entity defines the school fees policies.  Attributes include fee\_structure\_id, grade\_id, academic\_year, term\_id, fee\_type, amount, due\_date, and description. This entity makes use of unique constraints so as to prevent the duplication of fee definitions. 


\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Fee\_Structure} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{fee\_structure\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{grade\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{academic\_year} & YEAR \\
\texttt{term\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{fee\_type} & VARCHAR(50) \\
\texttt{amount} & DECIMAL(10,2) \\
\texttt{due\_date} & DATE \\
\texttt{description} & TEXT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:fee-structure-entity}
\end{table}

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Fee\_Transaction Entity}
The Fee\_Transaction entity captures all payment-related activities. It contains attributes such transaction\_id, student\_id, fee\_structure\_id, amount\_paid, balance\_due, payment\_date, status, and timestamp. The balance\_due attribute is derived as the difference between the required fee and the cumulative amount paid. This is obtained through a generated column.
\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Fee\_Transaction} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{transaction\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{student\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{fee\_structure\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{amount\_paid} & DECIMAL(10,2) \\
\texttt{balance\_due} & DECIMAL(10,2) \\
\texttt{payment\_date} & DATE \\
\texttt{status} & ENUM('Paid','Pending','Partially Paid') \\
\texttt{timestamp} & DATETIME \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:fee-transaction-entity}
\end{table}

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Subject Entity}
The Subject entity manages the academic curriculum content.  Its attributes include subject\_id, subject\_name, subject\_code, type (mandatory or elective), description, and credit\_hours. Uniqueness constraints on subject\_code ensure standardized classification across multiple academic levels. The system assumes that the school strictly adheres to the Examinations Council of Zambia (ECZ) and uses it as its only examination board. The constraint is that each subject can have multiple teachers..

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Subject} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{subject\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{subject\_name} & VARCHAR(100) \\
\texttt{subject\_code} & VARCHAR(20) \\
\texttt{type} & ENUM('Mandatory','Elective') \\
\texttt{description} & TEXT \\
\texttt{credit\_hours} & INT \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:subject-entity}
\end{table}
 %% A subsection
\subsection{Test\_Result Entity}
The Test\_Result entity captures the learner’s performance. Its attributes include student\_id, subject\_id, term\_id, test\_type, academic\_year, marks\_obtained, maximum\_mark, grade\_score and test\_date. Domain constraints restrict marks\_obtained from exceeding maximum\_marks, ensuring data accuracy in performance analytics. The grade\_score attribute is a derived attribute that outputs the symbolic grade (e.g., "Distinction", "Credit", or any other symbol) awarded for a student for an individual test. The assumption is that since the school uses ECZ for its national certifications, the grading system applied at all academic levels will strictly adhere to the official ECZ grading standards, including the use of their defined symbols and cut-off ranges. Constraint is that there are no duplicate test results for the same student, subject, test type and term.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Attributes of the \texttt{Test\_Result} Entity}
\begin{tabular}{l l }
\toprule
\textbf{Attribute} & \textbf{Data Type} \\
\midrule
\texttt{student\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{subject\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{term\_id} & INT \\
\texttt{test\_type} & VARCHAR(30) \\
\texttt{academic\_year} & YEAR \\
\texttt{marks\_obtained} & DECIMAL(5,2) \\
\texttt{maximum\_mark} & DECIMAL(5,2) \\
\texttt{grade\_score} & CHAR(2) \\
\texttt{test\_date} & DATE \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\label{tab:test-result-entity}
\end{table}

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Associative Entity}
The student\_guardian entity implements the many-to-many relationship between students and guardians. It includes student\_id and guardian\_id as foreign keys and is\_primary\_contact, which is a relationship attribute. Constraints enforce that each student must have at least one associated guardian.


