\section{Relationships and Cardinality}

Katikafwe Private School's operational structure and business rules are accurately modeled by the database schema. This schema includes a complex web of relationships. These relationships support the academic and administrative operations of the organization while enforcing cardinality constraints that preserve data integrity. In order to implement the business rule that specifies the school's structural organization through database constraints and application logic, the Grade and Class entities have a fundamental relationship that exhibits one-to-many cardinality. This is due to the fact that each grade includes exactly two classes and every class belongs to precisely one grade.

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Class-Student}
The relationship between Class and Student entities exhibits one-to-many cardinality. Each class contains multiple students, while each student is assigned to exactly one class per academic year. This relationship features mandatory participation for students, ensuring every learner that has been enrolled in the school has a designated instructional unit. 

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Student-Guardian}
The association between Student and Guardian entities represents many-to-many cardinality, implemented through the Student\_Guardian associative entity. This is because students may have multiple guardians while guardians may be responsible for multiple students. Particularly in familial contexts with siblings attending the same institution.

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Class-Subject}
The Class\_Subject associative entity, implements a ternary relationship between the Class, Subject, and Teacher entities. It reflects the institutional mandate that every subject offered to a particular class be paired with a qualified teacher. While individual teachers may be assigned to multiple class-subject combinations. This intricate relationship guarantees that every unique combination of class and subject has exactly one teacher assignment. 

 %% A subsection
\subsection{Student-Subject}
Many-to-many cardinality is also evident in the relationship between the Student and Subject entities. This is demonstrated through enrollment records that distinguish between selective enrollment and student interest. They exhibit a mandatory participation, which is automatically generated through class assignments.

 
%% A subsection
\subsection{Class-Subject}
Implemented by the Class\_Subject bridge entity, there is a ternary relationship between the Class, Subject, and Teacher entities. This relationship reflects the institutional requirement that every subject offered to a particular class be assigned to a qualified teacher. A teacher may be assigned to more than one combination of classes and subjects depending on the school's qualifications and needs. This relationship guarantees that each unique combination of class and subject has exactly one teacher assignment. Enrollment records that distinguish between required participation that is automatically generated through class assignments and selective enrollment that reflects students' academic choices and interests are another example of the many-to-many cardinality that is demonstrated in the relationship between the Student and Subject entities.

%% A subsection
\subsection{Class and Test\_results}

The relationship between Student and Test\_Result entities shows one-to-many cardinality, where each student may have multiple test results across different subjects, assessment types, and academic terms, while each test result is associated with exactly one student. This relationship ensures comprehensive academic performance tracking while maintaining referential integrity through foreign key constraints. The association between Term and Test\_Result entities exhibits one-to-many cardinality, with each term encompassing multiple assessment records while each test result belongs to precisely one academic term, enabling temporal organization of academic performance data.

%% A subsection
\subsection{Grade and Fee\_Structure}

The relationship between Grade and Fee\_Structure entities demonstrates one-to-many cardinality, where each grade may have multiple fee structures defined for different academic years and terms, while each fee structure is associated with exactly one grade level. This relationship supports the institution's financial management by enabling differentiated fee policies across grade levels. The association between Fee_Structure and Fee_Transaction entities shows one-to-many cardinality, with each fee structure generating multiple individual transactions as students fulfill their financial obligations, while each fee transaction references exactly one fee structure definition, ensuring consistency between institutional policies and individual financial records.

Additional relationships include the connection between Teacher and Grade entities for grade teacher assignments, between Teacher and Class entities for class teacher roles, and between Teacher and Class_Subject entities for subject teaching responsibilities. These relationships incorporate constraints preventing role conflicts, such as ensuring a teacher cannot serve as class teacher for multiple classes simultaneously within the same academic year, while allowing for flexible assignment patterns that accommodate the institution's staffing structure and operational requirements.%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
